r final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine which terms in this expansion are rational. A term is rational if both \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) are raised to integer powers that result in rational numbers. Specifically, \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is rational if \\(12-k\\) is even, and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is rational if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\(k\\) must satisfy both conditions: \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nLet's list the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b99c13840700fa36b3790f2d75c57baa9a7c7c2e73b3513a0864fb6724b12421"},"seconds":43.51847267150879,"seed":346765203911833118,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we use the binomial theorem:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nA term in this expansion is rational if both \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) are rational. This happens if \\(12-k\\) is even (so that \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is raised to an integer power) and \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3 (so that \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) is raised to an integer power).\n\nLet's list the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions:\n- \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3.\n- \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nThe multiples of 3 from 0 to 12 are: 0, 3, 6, 9, 12. We check which of these make \\(12-k\\) even:\n\n- If \\(k = 0\\), then \\(12-0 = 12\\) (even).\n- If \\(k = 3\\), then \\(12-3 = 9\\) (odd).\n- If \\(k = 6\\), then \\(12-6 = 6\\) (even).\n- If \\(k = 9\\), then \\(12-9 = 3\\) (odd).\n- If \\(k = 12\\), then \\(12-12 = 0\\) (even).\n\nSo, the valid values of \\(k\\) are 0, 6, and 12. We now calculate the corresponding terms:\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = 924 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81\n\\]\n\nAdding these rational terms together, we get:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we use the binomial theorem:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nA term in this expansion is rational if both \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) are rational. This happens if \\(12-k\\) is even (so that \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is raised to an integer power) and \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3 (so that \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) is raised to an integer power).\n\nLet's list the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions:\n- \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3.\n- \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nThe multiples of 3 from 0 to 12 are: 0, 3, 6, 9, 12. We check which of these make \\(12-k\\) even:\n\n- If \\(k = 0\\), then \\(12-0 = 12\\) (even).\n- If \\(k = 3\\), then \\(12-3 = 9\\) (odd).\n- If \\(k = 6\\), then \\(12-6 = 6\\) (even).\n- If \\(k = 9\\), then \\(12-9 = 3\\) (odd).\n- If \\(k = 12\\), then \\(12-12 = 0\\) (even).\n\nSo, the valid values of \\(k\\) are 0, 6, and 12. We now calculate the corresponding terms:\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = 924 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81\n\\]\n\nAdding these rational terms together, we get:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:445"}
